{{About|the active skill type|the support gem|Trap Support}}

A '''trap''' is a type of [[proxy]] and [[skill]] behaviour, similar to [[totem]]s and [[mine]]s. Trap [[skill]]s cause the player to throw a device that uses a skill, after being activated by a nearby enemy or by running out of trap duration. 

==Mechanics==
Traps are a form of [[proxy]], separate objects that use a skill in the player's stead. They have 1 maximum life, and will be destroyed if they take any damage. Traps cannot be damaged while they are traveling through the air, while they are arming, while they are triggering, or for 2 seconds after they have been thrown. While they cannot be targeted directly, they may be damaged by [[area of effect]] or [[reflect]]ed damage. Taking the [[Trap Mastery]] {{c|mod|Traps cannot be Damaged}} will prevent traps from being damaged at all. For most purposes, trap death does not affect their ongoing skill effects. 

By default, a character can have a maximum of 15 traps at a time. 

When a skill is simultaneously a trap, mine, and totem skill (e.g. {{il|Split Arrow}} supported by {{il|Trap Support}}, {{il|Blastchain Mine Support}}, {{il|Ballista Totem Support}}), the result is a skill that throws a mine, which throws a trap, which summons a [[totem]], which then uses the supported skill.

===Throwing===
When a trap skill is used, the character throws the trap to the target location. Characters have a base trap throwing speed of 0.6 seconds. Trap throwing speed may also scale specific trap skill effects, such as {{il|Seismic Trap}}. 

Skills with [[cooldown]]s consume one cooldown per trap, even when attempting to throw multiple at once. 

Throwing a trap that would exceed the maximum number of traps will cause the oldest trap to be destroyed. 

===Arming=== 
Once a thrown trap lands on the ground, it quickly arms itself. Trap duration determines how long each trap remains armed, after which they expire. Trap duration is specific to each trap skill, and is not affected by skill effect [[duration]] modifiers. Likewise, trap duration modifiers do not affect the duration of skill effects created by traps.

Armed traps are invisible to enemies.

===Triggering=== 
Armed traps can activate in two ways: by detecting an enemy inside their trigger radius, or by running out of trap duration. Trap activation is unrelated to the general [[trigger]] mechanic. 

Players have a base trap trigger radius of 1 [[distance|metre]],<ref name="Base Trigger Radius" /> scaled by modifiers to {{c|mod|Trap Trigger Area of Effect}}, but ''not'' skill area of effect modifiers. This range is a maximum: traps do not trigger against enemies outside the range of the skill they use, which may be smaller.<ref>https://www.pathofexile.com/forum/view-thread/1676002/filter-account-type/staff/page/2#p13452186</ref> 

If a trap activates at the end of its duration, but its skill requires a target (e.g. {{il|Bear Trap}}, {{il|Conversion Trap}}), the trap is destroyed without creating any effect.  

===Skill use=== 
Once triggered, traps ''use'' their skill. The skill itself is not triggered, and will not interact with modifiers for {{c|mod|Triggered Skills/Spells}}. 

As a form of [[proxy]], a trap uses the skill that was used to create it, with the offensive stats of its owner. This includes modifiers for [[damage]], [[area of effect]], [[debuff]]s, and most other on-hit effects. Note that on-hit effects that grant something, such as [[life]] or [[leech]] or [[charge]]s, will attempt to grant these effects to the trap, often uselessly. 

Traps do not possess non-offensive stats such as the player's [[action speed]] modifiers or most on-kill effects. They also cannot access skills other than the skill that was used to create them, meaning they cannot trigger secondary skills, or apply a [[Hex]] socketed in {{il|Mark of Submission}}. 

When a trap finishes its skill's attack or cast time, it either dies, or resets itself (e.g. {{il|Fire Trap of Blasting}}). Because traps create skill effects ''very'' early in their attack or cast animation,<ref>https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TlDDmCImZYI</ref> modifiers to attack or cast speed are often unimportant. They may be relevant for effects that require traps to be alive (e.g. non-skill [[curse]] on hit, {{il|The Retch}}) or to have finished their animation (e.g. [[repeat]]ing, {{c|mod|#% Chance for Traps to Trigger an additional time}}).

